# Problem Setup

# Reference Solution

# Literature

# Developer Notes

The standard regression cases use Amesos. With MORIS_HAVE_PETSC enabled,
additional Pressure_Vessel_3D_BDDC and Pressure_Vessel_3D_Immersed_BDDC Catch
cases select PETSc FGMRES, MATIS storage, and PCBDDC. They compare displacement,
temperature, and IQI output with the existing linear reference values.

CTest runs each BDDC case in a separate process on one and four MPI ranks
(one rank only when parallel tests are disabled or PETSc lacks MUMPS support).
CMake checks PETSc's configured MUMPS capability. This isolates PETSc's
initialization/finalization lifetime. Run the additional cases with:

\code
ctest --test-dir <build> -R '^Pressure_Vessel_3D(_Immersed)?-BDDC-' --output-on-failure
\endcode

All Amesos and BDDC CTest cases write to separate `cases/<test-name>/` directories.
The driver loads the input libraries from their absolute build output paths,
so concurrent tests cannot overwrite VIS temporary Exodus files or parameter
receipts. CTest accounts for each test's MPI process count when scheduling work.

BDDC output filenames contain the _BDDC suffix. The input files configure
BDDC through the SOL preconditioner parameters; CTest does not inject solver
settings through PETSC_OPTIONS. Explicit PETSc options can still override the
managed values for experiments.

The coupled displacement/temperature operator is nonsymmetric because of thermal
expansion, so FGMRES and bddc_symmetric = false are retained. Disconnected-subdomain
detection and vertex, edge, and face constraints are enabled. The input also sets
bddc_use_dof_splitting so UX, UY, UZ, and temperature have separate interface
constraints. Cartesian box corner metadata is not requested for the enriched cut
meshes. Ghost stabilization remains enabled and its terms use the same local
MATIS element-assembly path as the bulk terms.

Diagonal stiffness scaling is enabled as the baseline weighting. Serial BDDC
has no shared interfaces. On four ranks, deluxe scaling uses local interface
Schur complements in place of diagonal weights, with MUMPS for
pivoting Dirichlet, Neumann, and coarse factorizations. Both
bddc_sub_schurs_symmetric and bddc_sub_schurs_posdef are explicitly false: PETSc
3.23.2 otherwise independently assumes SPD sub-Schur problems, even when
bddc_symmetric is false, and enabling deluxe alone failed with a Cholesky zero
pivot. Serial tests retain PETSc's default local solvers and empty-coarse-problem
handling, without enabling deluxe.

The linear relative tolerance is 1e-13, with a restart of 50 and at most 2000
iterations. Shorter restarts control the drift between Krylov residual estimates
and the assembled residual in these ill-conditioned cut-cell systems; tightening
the linear tolerance alone with restart 1000 was insufficient. The Newton
relative residual threshold is 1e-8, matching the direct cases. A single Newton
update is followed by the usual residual convergence check; a second Newton-loop
entry for that check is not a second linear solve. Linear convergence reasons
are printed and failure to converge is an error. Numerical result checks retain
their existing 1e-5 tolerances. Existing Amesos tests retain their original solver
and filenames. In the verified one-/four-rank cases, each model needed one
linear solve and one Newton update: one Krylov iteration in serial and 125/123
iterations for the two parallel models in one diagnostic run.

All BDDC cases reuse check_results() with the same references as the direct
cases. In parallel the reference rank is still rank 1, but its report is forwarded
to rank 0 and printed there. The report is also saved to
`Pressure_Vessel_3D_Case_<index>_BDDC.check.log` in the case directory, so it can
be read when CTest hides successful-test output. Use `ctest -V` for live reports.
Failed comparisons still fail the Catch test after the report is transferred.

Before the restart/tolerance adjustment, the first four-rank immersed comparison
with PETSc 3.23.2 used multiplicity scaling with 885, 911, 923, and 924 linear
iterations (3643 total). Diagonal
stiffness scaling took 94, 112, 113, and 117 (436 total). Nonsymmetric deluxe
scaling took 70, 97, and 98 (265 total), requiring one fewer Newton correction.
The accumulated PETSc KSPSolve times were about 58.7, 3.3, and 3.0 seconds,
respectively, including the same true-residual monitoring in each comparison.
All three passed the original displacement, temperature, mesh, and IQI checks.
For Pressure_Vessel_3D on four ranks, the corresponding totals were 3450
(multiplicity), 429 (stiffness), and 256 (nonsymmetric deluxe); accumulated
KSPSolve times were about 57.9, 3.8, and 2.6 seconds. All passed their reference
checks. These are diagnostics from one machine/run, not iteration or timing guarantees.
Krylov residual estimates can differ from explicitly computed residuals in this
ill-conditioned system; assembled residual and field checks remain necessary. The checked-in inputs use one active material with E = 1 and
conductivity = 1 despite multiple geometry phases. Separate multi-material inputs
need their own verification; these measurements do not establish robustness for
arbitrary material contrasts or process counts.
*/
